r the  future because there's a housing  shortage it means that upper middle  class people move into what are  traditionally working-class  neighborhoods in order to afford a home  and that ends up making it difficult for  the traditional residents of this  neighborhoods to afford a place to live  in their own community that's what we  ended up is it then you know  middle-class housing has been converted  into rich people's housing and the  working man's housing has been turned  into middle class housing and then the  poor people's housing is kind of been  erased and it's actually important to  note that the gentrification professor  Myers is describing generally means it's  communities of color that are being  displaced to see what that looks like  firsthand I met up with a supercool  real-estate agent her name's Rose Garcia  and she works in all these developing  neighborhoods and she showed me around  them and we looked at these houses that  you know five years ago would cost  $100,000 and are now going for three or  four times that this home it's like less  than a thousand a thousand square feet I  think it's like 1,100 square feet it's  for sale for 305 and the problem is that  it's a total fiction and not to mention  the nice lot size again you're up  against an alleyway so this is the last  kind of home that the standard average  you know city employee teacher anyone  that makes around sixty five thousand  sixty thousand dollars a year this is  what you're getting you're getting an  extreme super fixer-upper in an area  that's in the bud the budding of its  gentrification oh and another thing  about the services there's a wild  chihuahua packs they just run  frivolously in the street just like  attacking people it's crazy you don't  have that on the west side no they don't  well welcome Cecily I'm born and raised  in Highland Park I'm born in 74 lived in  Highland Park my whole entire life I  still live there now and you know it's  amazing to me as an agent I